{
  "requirement": "Search results distinguish complete results from truncated or incomplete\nones. An operation failure returns an error, rather than a complete empty\nresult.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.sources.provider_results.search_completeness`.\n\nThe requirement has three obligations:\n1. Results must flag truncation, so `complete` is false only when a match was omitted.\n2. A complete result must really be complete, including when it is legitimately empty.\n3. An operation failure must return an error, not a complete empty result.\n\n**1. Truncated and complete results.**\n- `tests.rs#fn[completeness_is_false_only_when_a_match_is_omitted_and_errors_propagate]` runs text search over two matches. It uses limits `None`, `Some(2)`, `Some(1)` and `Some(0)`, and asserts both the match count and `complete`.\n  - Results are complete with no limit.\n  - Results are complete when the limit equals the match count.\n  - Results are incomplete when the limit is below the match count.\n- `tests.rs#fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` covers structural search with limits `None`, `Some(1)` and `Some(0)`. It asserts that `Some(0)` with one existing match is incomplete, and that `Some(1)` with one match is complete.\n- I checked the implementation only to see whether text and structural truncation use different logic. In `sources.rs#mod[defaults]/fn[search_text_with]` and `fn[search_structure_with]`, both set `complete: false` only when a further match is found at the limit. The structural tests therefore exercise the same decision as the text tests.\n\n**2. Genuinely empty results are complete.**\n- `completeness_is_false_only_when_a_match_is_omitted_and_errors_propagate` asserts that an \"absent\" pattern with `Some(0)` is complete and empty.\n- `tests.rs#fn[successful_empty_listing_and_empty_resources_are_distinct_from_errors]` covers an empty listing and empty resources. It asserts `complete && matches.is_empty()` for both text and structural search.\n- `tests.rs#fn[list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search]` asserts that a structural search whose language differs from the file's language is complete and empty. It also asserts that a successful search is complete.\n- `tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[empty_sources_errors_and_retained_snapshots_remain_distinct]` covers the real `Sources` provider. It asserts a successful empty listing, a truncated text search with `limit(1)` that is incomplete, and errors for the cases below.\n  - A missing path.\n  - A listing that fails.\n  - An invalid pattern.\n\n**3. Failures return errors.**\n- `tests.rs#fn[failed_provider_operations_never_become_successful_empty_results]` uses `FailingProvider` with a failing `list` and then a failing `read`. It asserts that `search_text`, `parse` and `search_structure` each return an error, and it checks the exact error messages.\n- `completeness_is_false_only_when_a_match_is_omitted_and_errors_propagate` covers an invalid regex, invalid UTF-8 parse and unknown language parse.\n- `list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search` covers a malformed structural rule, an unknown query language, and a provider that returns a different resource from `parse`. All of these must return errors.\n\nI found no unacknowledged gap and no obligation that lacks a test with an assertion.",
  "references": "712ef770c6acc57e62fd03986770022957d9e862253527652dd7cad64b53edd4",
